 **Plain English.**
 - TS-a: every true predictor whose signal the other predictors cannot imitate ends up with inclusion probability near one.
 - TS-b: the posterior concentrates on the exact true model. The highest-probability and median-probability models both equal the true model with probability tending to one, and every inclusion probability is near 1 for true predictors and near 0 for nulls.
-- The condition for TS-b: leaving out any group of true predictors must leave more unexplained signal than twice what the prior and the Occam factor charge for those predictors, with a little room to spare. When nulls are added in their place, there is an extra allowance for the best-looking nulls among p; this allowance is at most about √(2m log p) and estimated much smaller on S1's AR(1) designs (0.1–0.6, E9). Rivals whose prior-plus-Occam saving from the added nulls already exceeds the deletion price need no condition at all; at S1's sizes that saving is about zero, so this matters only asymptotically.
